--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
static_assert condition could not be evaluated
dropped the body
--
Two defects.  The parser took the ptr-operator of `auto *f()` / `auto &f()`
(stored, like a trailing return type, in the function type's subtype) for a
trailing return type and moved it into the declaration type, losing the
placeholder ("invalid implicit conversion ... to '<<type:nil>> &'"); a
trailing return type is now marked as such.  cpp_convert_auto rebuilt the
substituted type without its qualifiers, so `auto &f() { return ci; }` and
`auto &r = ci` deduced `int &` for a `const int` (the variable path patched
const back afterwards; the function path did not).  Also covers the deferred
deduction when the return expression names a body-local variable, member
functions, const `this`, volatile, pointer-to-pointer, and a range-for with
`const auto &`.  Non-vacuous: the last assertion states a wrong value and
must FAIL.
